{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run inventory-api on node1 as a managed service listening on TCP port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The systemd unit was enabled and active, Python listened on 0.0.0.0:8080, and both health and inventory endpoints returned valid responses. The same state was directly verified after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bd64fdbb22c24989","cmd-8077d1f7ea664b40","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Limit each batchworker process to 128 MB of virtual memory and ensure a failed batch allocation does not interrupt the API.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Fresh batchworker sessions showed matching soft and hard address-space limits of 131072 KiB (128 MiB). A 256 MiB allocation raised MemoryError while the API remained active, healthy, and on the same PID; this isolation behavior was demonstrated both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f622218d25254841","cmd-51235168b30e4b57","cmd-8077d1f7ea664b40"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"AlmaLinux 9.6 and kernel 5.14.0-570.12.1.el9_6.x86_64 were unchanged from baseline through the final post-restart observation. The API ran successfully using the platform-provided /usr/bin/python3.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ad6fea991b5343ee","cmd-6701fa4eea1349bf","cmd-8077d1f7ea664b40","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"All managed-system mutations had a clear task or mandatory restart-evidence purpose: account creation, API installation, systemd management, the batchworker limit, bounded allocation tests, and the node restart. The malformed first allocation test did not show a state change and was corrected. No failed services or package damage were observed. No causally unnecessary mutation was identified, but two transient test-output files were left in /tmp rather than cleaned up: /tmp/batch-allocation.out from cmd-51235168b30e4b57 and /tmp/post-reboot-allocation.out from cmd-8077d1f7ea664b40. This is minor retained residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4fefe720fcd44bb1","cmd-6701fa4eea1349bf","cmd-9418a50b72e94b72","cmd-f622218d25254841","cmd-51235168b30e4b57","cmd-2492d03ad0994808","cmd-8077d1f7ea664b40","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart scheduling was acknowledged, the connection interruption and changed boot ID demonstrated an actual reboot, and the service, endpoint data, memory limits, allocation isolation, OS, and kernel were healthy afterward. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart. Only minor temporary-file residue affects hygiene.","limitations":["Lifecycle snapshots inventory temporary-file paths but not their contents or size; the retained test-output files are therefore assessed as minor residue based on the command trace.","No material restart-evidence gaps or contradictions were found."]}
